  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows rain showers \nlightening up across east central Florida, however, moderate to \nheavy rainfall offshore over the local Atlantic waters are moving \nonshore. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local observations indicate\ncloudy skies across east central Florida. Analysis charts show a\nweak trough over low pressure near the southeast coast of \nFlorida. Winds are breezy from the east at 10-20mph with gusts up \nto 30-35mph, especially along the coast. Temperatures are \ncurrently in the upper 60s to upper 70s with dew points in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. Widespread showers are forecast tonight \nwhich will be more persistent near the coast from Indian River to \nVolusia counties compared to inland locations. There is a High \nRisk (level 4 of 4) for excessive rainfall with the potential for \nflash flooding across much of Brevard county and portions of \nVolusia, as well as Indian River counties with a Slight to \nModerate Risk elsewhere. Do not drive through flooded roadways; \nturn around, don't drown! Expect dangerous coastal and beach \nhazards with high surf and a High Risk of life-threatning rip \ncurrents. \n\nFriday-Saturday... The persistently wet pattern will continue to\nend the workweek and into the weekend as low pressure develops\nover the southwest Atlantic waters Friday into Saturday. The\nNational Hurricane Center has outlooked the southwest Atlantic\nwith a 60% chance of tropical development. There is still quite a\nbit of uncertainty in the forecast (especially for this close in\ntime) with guidance continuing to go back and forth on the \nstrength, as well as the track of the  low which would determine \nwhere the highest additional rainfall totals will occur. The low \nis forecast to develop over the southwest Atlantic and strengthen \nas it tracks north-northeast across the local Atlantic waters and \neventually to the northeast of the Florida coast. Numerous to \nwidespread onshore moving showers (~60-90% chance) are forecast on\nFriday which will be off and on at times. \n\nScattered to numerous instances of total additional rainfall \namounts of 4-6\"+ are forecast from Thursday afternoon to Friday \nevening; a reasonable worst case scenario (1 in 10 chance) for \nisolated rainfall amounts to 8-10\"+ with the greatest potential \nfor the highest rainfall amounts from Volusia county to Indian \nRiver county are forecast. For this reason, the Weather Prediction\nCenter has placed east central Florida in a Slight to High Risk\nfor excessive rainfall on Friday. Rain chances decrease on \nSaturday on the backside of the low with isolated to numerous \nshowers forecast. Expect breezy conditions from the northeast at \n10-20mph with gusts to 30-35mph. Expect dangerous coastal and beach \nhazards with high surf and a High Risk of life-threatning rip \ncurrents. Seasonable highs in the mid 70s to low 80s with lows\nin the mid 60s to low 70s are forecast.\n\nSunday-Wednesday... Deterministic models and LREF-ensemble \nclusters are in good agreement for through Tuesday before\ndiverging in potential solutions on Wednesday. Regardless, drier \nair is expected to continue to filter across east central Florida \non Sunday as 250mb flow becomes zonal over east central Florida \nthrough Tuesday. Guidance then shows a mid/upper level low \ndeepening over the Deep South on Wednesday. Offshore flow on \nSunday is forecast to gradually shift onshore through Tuesday and \nthen northerly on Wednesday. Moisture is expected to increase \n(PWATs up to 1.0-1.7\") over east central Florida as a result \nTuesday and Wednesday. Isolated to scattered showers are forecast \nwith a low chance of lightning on Tuesday. Seasonable temperatures\nare forecast.",
